          "title": "You'll want to know: disserō, disserere",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "disserō, disserere — \"to discuss, to hold forth (on)\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Senex dē amīcitiā disseruit. The old man spoke at length about friendship."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "disserō, disserere, disseruī, dissertum. Dē + ablative names the subject. It is a different verb from serō, to sow. English dissertation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "disserō is built from dis- \"apart\" and serō, serere, seruī \"to join, to string together\" (Lewis & Short), a different verb from serō \"to sow\": to set ideas out in a row. English dissertation comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
